WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.849
Turning to chapter 2, advanced problems, problem 

00:00:02.849 --> 00:00:05.640
number 10 asks this seemingly innocent question.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.468
Find the number of subsets of the set 1 up to 2000, 

00:00:09.468 --> 00:00:12.420
the sum of whose elements is divisible by 5.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.833
That might take a little moment to parse, but to illustrate let me 

00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:18.920
show it on a much simpler example, where we have the set 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.640
This has 2 to the 5th, or 32 distinct subsets, which look like this.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.479
If you compute the sum of each of those subsets, 

00:00:26.479 --> 00:00:29.374
and you organize them by which ones have the same sum, 

00:00:29.374 --> 00:00:33.060
you'll see that 8 of those subsets have a sum which is divisible by 5.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.920
So the answer here would be 8.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.700
The case with all the subsets of 1 up to 2000 is obviously way, 

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.896
way bigger, and you might be able to guess at a rough estimate for the answer, 

00:00:42.896 --> 00:00:45.500
but the challenge is to compute the exact answer.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.713
I really love this problem, and the full video I did on this question 

00:00:49.713 --> 00:00:53.320
covers a really beautiful and surprising way you can think about it.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.217
It uses one of those tools that mathematicians keep in their 

00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:59.180
belt that legitimately feels like magic the first time you see it.

